## Migration: `Thread.Abort` Timeout → Cooperative Cancellation (.NET 8)

### Problem

In .NET 6+, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` (SYSLIB0006). `Thread.ResetAbort()` is also gone. Your code uses the **timeout enforcement** pattern — it must be replaced with cooperative cancellation.

### Pattern Classification

| API | Status in .NET 8 |
|-----|-------------------|
| `Thread.Abort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` |
| `ThreadAbortException` | Never thrown by the runtime |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` |

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);

        try
        {
            // Task.WaitAsync throws OperationCanceledException if token fires
            return task.WaitAsync(cts.Token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
        catch (AggregateException ex) when (ex.InnerExceptions.Count == 1)
        {
            throw new AggregateException(ex.InnerException!);
        }
    }

    // Preferred: async overload for new callers
    public async Task<T> ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);

        try
        {
            var task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);
            return await task.WaitAsync(cts.Token);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }

    // Best: pass the token into the work itself for true cooperative cancellation
    public async Task<T> ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var token = cts.Token;

        try
        {
            return await Task.Run(() => work(token), token);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }
}
```

### Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Explanation |
|---------|-------------|
| **Work doesn't actually stop** | Unlike `Thread.Abort`, cancellation is *cooperative*. If `work` doesn't check the token, it keeps running in the background even after `TimeoutException` is thrown. The caller moves on, but the thread pool thread is still busy. |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | Leaks timers. Always use `using`. |
| **`Task.WaitAsync` vs the actual task** | `WaitAsync` unblocks the *waiter* on cancellation, but the underlying task continues running. This matches your original semantics (caller gets `TimeoutException`) but the work isn't killed. |
| **Catching `OperationCanceledException` too broadly** | Use `when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)` to distinguish your timeout from external cancellation. |

### Making Work Truly Cancellable

For the timeout to actually *stop* work (not just unblock the caller), refactor `work` to accept a `CancellationToken`:

```csharp
// Before (uncooperative — work keeps running after timeout)
executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(() => ExpensiveCalculation(), TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));

// After (cooperative — work stops at checkpoints)
executor.ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync(token =>
{
    foreach (var item in items)
    {
        token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // checkpoint
        Process(item);
    }
    return result;
}, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
```

### If Work Cannot Be Modified (Uncooperative Code)

If `work` is a black-box call (third-party library, long-running native call), cooperative cancellation won't help. Move it to a **child process** and use `Process.Kill()`:

```csharp
// Last resort for truly uncooperative code
var process = Process.Start("worker.exe", args);
if (!process.WaitForExit(timeout))
{
    process.Kill(entireProcessTree: true);
    throw new TimeoutException(...);
}
```

This is the only way to replicate the "forcible termination" semantics of `Thread.Abort` in modern .NET.
